I now have a comprehensive picture of the latest AI developments from multiple leading tech news sources (TechCrunch, The Verge, VentureBeat, and Google's official blog). Here's the summary:

## Latest Developments in Artificial Intelligence

### 🚀 New Model Releases & Product Launches
- **Gemini 3.7 Flash (Google)**: Google's newest model is now available in AI Mode for Search and powers "Gemini Spark." It offers substantial improvements in software engineering, web development workflows, tool use for Google Workspace, and instruction following. It's also proving more competitive in coding and agent workloads at a lower price tier.
- **Grok 4.6 (xAI)**: Launched with "frontier-level intelligence," large gains over the previous generation, and stronger long-running agent behavior. xAI also debuted **Grok Bot**, a workforce-style interface combining computer use, persistence, workflow learning, and multi-agent coordination.
- **OpenAI "Ultrafast" mode**: A new mode that makes **GPT-5.6 Sol** operate at 14x speed.
- **Writer's Palmyra X6**: New AI model with an upgraded "harness" reporting 52% lower costs, 48% faster speed, and 10% better quality for agent tasks.
- **Claude Cowork (Anthropic)**: Its Chrome extension was upgraded into "Claude Cowork," syncing chats across desktop, web, and mobile apps.

### 💼 Major Business & Funding Moves
- **Stripe reportedly acquiring OpenRouter** for $7B+ (AI gateway startup).
- **SpaceX closed its $60B acquisition of Cursor**, the AI coding tool, which will now work on Grok.
- **Databricks** raised $5B at a **$190B valuation** (investors wanted to give $15B).
- **Wispr** raised $280M at a $2B valuation for AI dictation.
- **Lovable** confirmed a $13.3B valuation, raising another $400M.
- **OpenAI-backed Thrive Holdings** raised $2B for enterprise AI.
- **Cognition** (AI coding startup) reportedly in talks to raise at a **$40B valuation**.
- **Mistral AI** announced an ambitious plan to build **1 gigawatt of European AI compute by 2030** (~$38B in capital).
- **IBM partnered with OpenAI** to bolster enterprise AI.
- **Nvidia** unveiled a new ~$500B plan, seen as risky but strategic for aging GPU infrastructure.
- **OpenAI's enterprise revenue has surpassed its consumer revenue** (CFO Sarah Friar reportedly told investors).

### 🏛️ Safety, Regulation & Policy
- **Anthropic's AI watermarking**: Anthropic detailed how Claude's invisible text watermarks work, using a version of Google's open-source **SynthID-Text** system. Some users are unhappy about detection in workplace/academic settings.
- **OpenAI reportedly disbanded its preparedness team** — the latest shakeup of its safety teams as it heads toward an IPO. OpenAI also lost two executives in one week and hired a new CRO.
- **Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei** said the AI backlash is "fundamentally a crisis of trust."
- **AI safety debate**: Three AI pioneers made the case for staying open as safety concerns mount; a piece on "rogue AI" argued such scenarios are no longer just science fiction.
- **Anthropic experiment**: AI agents set loose on the same task started a "turf war" with each other.

### 🛠️ Enterprise & Developer News
- **Microsoft** is killing off unsuccessful AI features and merging its separate Copilot apps into a "super app"; its Clippy-like "Mico" character is no longer the face of Copilot.
- **Google Meet** now has AI notetaking for in-person meetings ("Take notes for me" with transcripts and action items).
- **YouTube's "Ask YouTube"** conversational AI search is rolling out to mobile.
- **Z.ai** reported 2,436 vulnerability findings across 269 projects in collaboration with Chinese security teams.
- **Google DeepMind** experienced a leadership shakeup, prompting questions about whether Google wants to win at AI.
- **Google I/O 2026** theme: "the agentic Gemini era" — the Gemini app now has **1 billion+ monthly users**.
- Google's **AMIE medical AI system** demonstrated real-time clinical video consultation in a first-of-its-kind study.

### ⚖️ Controversies & Ethical Issues
- **Twitch** will train Amazon's generative AI models on streamers' content **by default** (opt-out only); the CPO admitted "if it was opt-in, nobody would opt in."
- **Apple** is in talks to pay publishers to improve AI-powered Siri with current news, and trained its own AI model for China with help from **Alibaba**.
- **Google** now lets users remove visible watermarks from its AI-generated images.
- A lawsuit alleges a man used **Grok** to transform a childhood photo into explicit imagery.
- **AI data center boom backlash**: Communities across the US are pushing back on data center projects (bans, lawsuits, political pressure), and hyperscalers' pivot to natural gas is drawing scrutiny.

### 📈 Notable Trends
- **Agentic AI** is the dominant theme — from Google's "agentic Gemini era" to Grok Bot, Claude Cowork, and enterprise agent tooling (Harness).
- **Model cost optimization** is a key battleground — companies are finding ways to slash inference costs (Kog, Writer, Google's cheaper Flash tier).
- **AI coding tools** are consolidating at enormous valuations (Cursor→SpaceX, Cognition at $40B).
- **Enterprise AI adoption** is accelerating, with enterprise revenue now exceeding consumer revenue at OpenAI.

---

*Note: Some sources contain dates into 2026 (Google I/O 2026, "Made by Google '26" Pixel 11 event), reflecting the news sites' current reporting cycles. The search API was rate-limited during this session, so the summary is drawn primarily from fetched live pages of TechCrunch, The Verge, VentureBeat, and Google's official blog.*
